#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:5
#: e9fab6362e7e4aa681260258e18b58c6
msgid "Multiple Radio Control Receivers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:7
#: bf14a46a067b4450ad35c30992d9a0f4
msgid "It is possible to use multiple radio control receivers in ArduPilot for redundancy or hand-off between multiple transmitters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:10
#: 565c28dbbe7b497cbffd43a5532fe8a2
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:12
#: 2dc8c551d6af492b93d43f9a308ca3f1
msgid "This configuration only is valid for autopilots that use an IOMCU co-processor. Check the product description to be sure that the autopilot includes either an STMF100 or STMF103 co-processor. Usually these outputs have servo/motor outputs labeled as MAIN (the co-processor) and AUX (from the main cpu)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:14
#: 448e29a5b5354b8990f777e677363101
msgid "The additional receivers can be attached to any Serial Port's RX input as long as they use a serial protocol. These are: SBUS, FPort, DSM, DSM2, DSM-X, IBus, SUMD, and SRXL (and CRSF if the TX output of the UART is also used)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:17
#: 8ee0cc579c0b4708a492c1f9bd04f52f
msgid "For whichever Serial Port the receiver is attached, the ``SERIALx_PROTOCOL`` should be set to 23. The baud rate is ignored and auto-detected, as well as the type of the receiver."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:19
#: d4868af4996a40dab7dc00537a2da73a
msgid "SBUS and FPort must either be externally inverted or the SERIALx_OPTIONS be set for inversion (F and H processor types only are capable of this)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:21
#: 594343bb53054a668c78be5b9bb2f170
msgid "The :ref:`RC_OPTIONS<RC_OPTIONS>` bit 10 must be set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:26
#: d4506acdfa9a4260958f384de1500f7e
msgid "Failsafe and Changeover"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:28
#: 9e6683ec4a9849338d418331426b24db
msgid "The autopilot will always use the receiver attached to the IOMCU's RCin, if providing valid RC signals. If that receiver fails to provide valid RC signals, it will then switch to the other receiver if it is outputting valid RC signals. It will reverted to the first if it restarts outputting valid RC signals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:30
#: a5016c42eac246cb9a3ef9eef5744dcb
msgid "Note that the previously calibrated ``RCx_MAX`` , ``RCx_MIN``, ``RCx_REVERSED``, ``RCx_DZ`` and ``RCx_TRIM`` parameters for each channel will be used by the active receiver and could vary between receivers, especially if different types are mixed. It is recommended that all receivers be of the same manufacture and type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:32
#: c565fb2d88a44d5ca408c7ce3509b5e7
msgid "lowering throttle to below :ref:`FS_THR_VALUE<FS_THR_VALUE>`  on the first receiver will not force a changeover, but rather force a normal radio failsafe. Only loss of RC signals from the first receiver will cause a changeover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:34
#: 0b2984f7173645a9be32f6b99cab85e0
msgid "If all receivers become inactive (no pulses), then the normal radio failsafe handling will begin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:36
#: d61dd4ece6e849288a34e9d762918a31
msgid "It is important that all receivers be configured for no pulses for radio loss failsafe for the changeovers to work properly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:39
#: 54b1ba7740b641a0a4eb3e9aff6b1552
msgid "Inversion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:41
#: e1f9f386fcf348a29e46fc71cdb52c26
msgid "Some protocols, like SBus, require that the signal be inverted . Autopilots using F7/H7 cpus have the ability to invert the RX input via its ``SERIALx_OPTIONS`` bitmask. Otherwise an external inverter must be used between the receiver data output and the Serial Port's RX input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:44
#: bac98918090c4790b07721802b1e920d
msgid "Receivers Having Telemetry/Video Transmitters Integrated"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:46
#: 7a2a74b8eaa8475cba1fe654f67619fe
msgid "Some receivers have telemetry and/or video transmitters integrated. Having multiple units, operating on the same band, in the same vehicle, can cause reduction of range in both units, even if using spread-spectrum techniques. CRSF, FrSky, DragonLink, etc. are some examples of these types of receivers. Having the antennas as widely separated as possible will help, but not eliminate some range reduction. Best case is to have the units operating in different radio bands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-multiple-rx.rst:48
#: f7edab1146aa4b4aa9b33338415a3cf9
msgid "Be sure to do range checks on the ground using the transmitter's range check mode before flight testing."
msgstr ""
